          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Apart from medication, what helps with long-term insomnia?",
          "answer": "Cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I) is the first-line non-drug treatment. The guideline summary says it is recommended ahead of medication for chronic insomnia in adults, and its benefits tend to last after treatment ends. It is usually delivered over about four to eight sessions. The passage I found names two parts: **stimulus control** (using the bed only for sleep and getting up when unable to sleep) and **sleep restriction** (limiting time in bed, then gradually extending it). The results cut off before listing the other components. (Source: *Chronic insomnia: cognitive behavioral therapy as first-line treatment*)\n\nDaily habits may also matter. The lifestyle summary says caffeine can keep people awake even when they feel it does not affect them, so an early-afternoon cut-off is a common approach. It describes alcohol as shortening the time to fall asleep but breaking up the second half of the night, and nicotine withdrawal as contributing to early waking. It also says a short early-afternoon nap is compatible with good night sleep for most people, while long or late naps can reduce sleep pressure. (Source: *Sleep habits for insomnia*)\n\nThe retrieved material does not say how well these habits work on their own. The insomnia overview says that sleep hygiene advice alone is not effective treatment, but the passage that explains this was cut off, so I cannot tell you more.\n\nSome signs mean insomnia is not the only issue and need a clinician's assessment. These include loud snoring with witnessed pauses in breathing, excessive daytime sleepiness, falling asleep while driving, acting out dreams, and sleep problems alongside low mood or hopelessness. (Source: *Insomnia*) If you have any of these, please speak with a clinician.\n\nFor a plan that fits you, talk with a doctor or other clinician, who can ask about your situation and point you to CBT-I providers.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
